<urn:atm:triage:finding/AW2-QA-HARNESS-001>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "AW2-QA-HARNESS-001" ;
  triage:title "run_admission_capacity.py silently reuses a stale target/release/atm-daemon-benchmark probe across commits" ;
  triage:description "fenix (qa-pr1179-r6 bench-smoke run, msg 01M1QTFT87SNFXNTZZ4MR7GK1R) on rand-m5/atmbench: scripts/smoke/run_admission_capacity.py:875 only builds target/release/atm-daemon-benchmark when the file is missing, not when it is stale relative to the commit under test. Observed once here (Aug 23 stale probe vs 5a4bdefc7 target) as a hard usage-mismatch failure, so this instance did not silently corrupt a result -- but a probe binary that happens to remain command-line-compatible across commits would be silently reused and would benchmark the wrong writer code, producing a false parity/regression verdict. fenix worked around it this run by rebuilding the probe per commit before measuring. quality-mgr ruling: important, not blocking -- this run's 5a4bdefc7 vs 1106a5db4 parity result (sqlite p50 46838 vs 46402 +0.9%, uds p50 21577 vs 21576 flat) is valid because the workaround (explicit per-commit rebuild) was applied and confirmed before measuring. Per the benchmark-infra freeze, this is a behavioral-rule fix (always rebuild/verify probe freshness against the commit under test, e.g. compare probe mtime or embedded build sha vs HEAD before running), not new benchmark harness machinery; does not require a new sprint. Tracked as a standing repeatable-pattern risk for every future bench-smoke/official run using this script until fixed." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-aw" ;
  triage:triageMode "reported" ;
  triage:category "test-infra" ;
  triage:severity "important" ;
  triage:repeatable true ;
  triage:sweepScope "workspace" ;
  triage:status "fixed" ;
  triage:dispatchReady false ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-09-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:foundIn triage:AW2 ;
  triage:foundAt "2026-09-05T03:41:10Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AW2-QA-HARNESS-001/AW2-1> ;
  triage:closedAt "2026-09-05T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:closedBy "quality-mgr" ;
  triage:triageRecordVersion "3" .

# CLOSURE (qa-pr1179-r7, quality-mgr): dev's fix at d3a601ab0 removed the
# stale-probe early-return in scripts/smoke/run_admission_capacity.py's
# sqlite_writer_probe (now rebuilds unconditionally every call, closing the
# staleness gap this finding described), but that same fix broke the
# pre-existing mocked unit test
# scripts/smoke/test_run_admission_capacity.py::
# test_sqlite_probe_build_is_separate_from_the_released_daemon_pair (it
# assumed the removed two-is_file()-call structure) -- a genuine CI-breaking
# regression on Just-lint (ubuntu-latest, macos-latest), confirmed by
# quality-mgr via CI run 33947118389 job 101255129239 and independently
# confirmed by req-qa this round. At the subsequent merge-forward tip
# f77ed7991e (feature/aw2-sqlite-diagnostic-timeline <- integrate/phase-aw),
# a companion fix updated the test to return_value=True and assert
# command.call_count == 2 (two sqlite_writer_probe() calls, each rebuilding),
# matching the corrected single-is_file()-per-call production behavior.
# quality-mgr independently re-ran
# `python3 -m pytest scripts/smoke/test_run_admission_capacity.py -k
# test_sqlite_probe_build_is_separate_from_the_released_daemon_pair` at
# f77ed7991e in /tmp/verify-aw2-r7 and confirmed PASSED. Both the original
# staleness defect and its own fix's regression are now closed.

<urn:atm:triage:occurrence/AW2-QA-HARNESS-001/AW2-1>
